Title: "Subsets"
Id: "d1d71d30-1ce6-427e-bbc4-dd3ccf53fe53"


Text: "One set is a subset of another when every element of the first is also an
       element of the second. Subsethood is the basic way of comparing sets by
       inclusion, and the power set axiom depends on it."
Id: "7bfbdad3-bf51-4e2d-ae06-8dd2fa3a220d"


[A \:subset:/ B]
Declares: A is \set
when: B is \set
satisfies:
. forAll: X "in" A
  then: X "in"? B
Requires:
. capability: x_ "in" A :-> x_ "in" B
Documented:
. called: "$A?$ is a subset of $B?$"
. written: "A? \subseteq B?"
. description: "$A \subseteq B$ holds when every element of $A$ is also an
                element of $B$. By extensionality, two sets are equal exactly
                when each is a subset of the other."
Id: "2b2b89a1-d106-4377-ae48-14c704063e5e"


Text: "A subset that is not the whole set is called a *proper* subset: it is
       contained in the larger set but differs from it."
Id: "c27fbf42-311e-4efd-b4c8-2da3ad6370a7"


[A \:(proper)::subset:/ B]
Refines: A
when: B is \set
specifies: A \:subset:/ B
satisfies: A != B
Documented:
. adjective: "proper"
. written: "A? \subsetneq B?"
. description: "$A \subsetneq B$ holds when $A$ is a subset of $B$ but is not
                equal to $B$; that is, $B$ has at least one element not in $A$."
Id: "7d070f37-61a5-4c62-b8da-4831c222e4b2"


Text: "Subsethood is transitive: a subset of a subset is a subset of the whole.
       This is the first small theorem of the collection, and it follows
       directly from the definition of subset."
Id: "370b0f76-ab0f-4f7f-80fa-120bb7ffe9e2"


Theorem:
given:
. C is \set
. B \:subset:/ C
. A \:subset:/ B
then:
. A \:subset?:/ C
Documented:
. called: "Transitivity of Subset Relation"
. description: "If $A \subseteq B$ and $B \subseteq C$, then $A \subseteq C$."
Proof: """Suppose {.A \:subset?:/ B.} and {.B \:subset?:/ C.} and let
          {.x "in" A.}. Then {.A \:subset?:/ B.} implies {.x "in"? B.} and
          {.B \:subset?:/ C.} implies {.x "in"? C.}.

          Since {.x.} was arbitrary, we have shown for all {.x "in"? A.} then
          {.x "in"? C.}. Thus {.A \:subset?:/ C.}."""
Id: "6a4bcb50-97bc-4d1b-a9b2-750c963bb720"


Text: "The characterization of equality by mutual inclusion, promised when
       extensionality was stated, is recorded here as a theorem: two sets are
       equal exactly when each is a subset of the other."
Id: "a0b6d62f-1f1d-4be5-997b-40aa03cc4ac7"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
then: A = B
iff:
. A \:subset?:/ B
. B \:subset?:/ A
Documented:
. called: "Set Equality via Mutual Inclusion"
. description: "Two sets $A$ and $B$ are equal if and only if $A \subseteq B$
                and $B \subseteq A$."
Id: "d3ee9362-d0be-47f0-bf0e-ecea7416d100"


SectionTitle: "The Inclusion Order"
Id: "2c6cb7fb-49cd-41a9-8ce3-c143e7cc045c"


[\inclusion.order:on{U}]
Defines: R is \partial.order:on{\power.set:of{U}}
when: U is \set
expresses:
. forAll: A, B "in" \power.set:of{U}
  then:
  . have: R(A, B)
    iff: A \:subset?:/ B
. exists: E "in" \power.set:of{U}
  suchThat:
  . E = \empty.set
  . forAll: A "in" \power.set:of{U}
    then: R(E, A)
. exists: T "in" \power.set:of{U}
  suchThat:
  . T = U
  . forAll: A "in" \power.set:of{U}
    then: R(A, T)
Documented:
. called: "inclusion order on the power set of $U?$"
. description: "Subset inclusion is a partial order on $\mathcal{P}(U)$, with
                the empty set as its least element and $U$ as its greatest
                element."
Id: "ff6d7e2f-1927-4d30-b771-34ea8726711c"


[\family.of.subsets:of{U}]
Declares: A \:subset:/ \power.set:of{U}
when: U is \set
Documented:
. called: "family of subsets of $U?$"
. description: "A family of subsets of $U$ is a subset
                $\mathcal{A}\subseteq\mathcal{P}(U)$. Thus $x\in A$ concerns an
                element of one subset, while $A\in\mathcal{A}$ concerns one set
                in the family."
Id: "df5630a9-0ac4-4920-995a-7002bdccbf15"
